Note: `SqliteConnection` objects should stay alive per context — the in-memory database lives as long as the connection. To be safe, keep the connection alive by returning it alongside the context, or use this small fixture change:

```csharp
private (BlogContext Database, DbConnection Connection) GivenDatabase()
{
    var connection = new SqliteConnection("Data Source=:memory:");
    connection.Open();
    var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder<BlogContext>()
        .UseSqlite(connection)
        .Options;
    var database = new BlogContext(options);
    database.Database.EnsureCreated();
    return (database, connection);
}
```

The context disposes but each test creates a fresh context/connection pair, so the simpler first version works as long as `BlogContext` doesn't close the connection passed via `UseSqlite` — with `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite`, the context does not own an externally opened connection, so the first version is safe since `connection` is captured by `options` indirectly... actually it isn't; assign it to a field or keep a reference. Safer version shown in the fixture above — call it via `var (database, connection) = GivenDatabase();` and add `using var _ = connection;`.

